  "id": "marchinha_carnaval",
  "name": "Marchinha (Rio carnival march-song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Brisk carnival march-song of Rio de Janeiro, conventionally traced to Chiquinha Gonzaga's Ó Abre Alas (1899), that dominated the carnival hits issued on 78 rpm records through the 1930s, 1940s and 1950s, the decades when composers such as Lamartine Babo and Braguinha wrote new marchinhas for each season. Records paired a singer and chorus with a studio orchestra of trumpets, trombones and reeds over samba percussion, and the songs remain the repertoire of street carnival bands.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1930s marchinha, carnival Processional, Brazilian branch. trumpet, trombone, clarinet, samba foundation cavaquinho, surdo, goatskin canonical Brazilian premium eucalyptus imbuia canonical Brazilian luthier brass platinelas canonical Brazilian pandeiro. blanket damped 1925-1945 commercial-major electrical era office. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
